      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "New tests, such as magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and electron-beam computed tomography (CT), are being developed for the diagnosis of coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the conditions that a new test must meet to be a cost-effective alternative to established imaging tests."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Decision model and cost-effectiveness analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
          "content": "Literature review and meta-analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TARGET POPULATION",
          "content": "55-year-old men and 65-year-old women presenting with chest pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TIME HORIZON",
          "content": "Lifetime of the patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "PERSPECTIVE",
          "content": "Health care policy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "MRI, electron-beam CT, exercise echocardiography, exercise single-photon emission CT, and coronary angiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Target sensitivity and specificity values for a new noninvasive test."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS OF BASE-CASE ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Assuming that society is willing to pay $75000 per quality-adjusted life-year (QALY) gained, a new test that costs $1000 would need a sensitivity of 94% and a specificity of 90% to be cost-effective."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS OF SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Assuming that society is willing to pay $50000 per QALY gained, a new test that costs $1000 or more would never be cost-effective. For a test that costs $500, the sensitivity and specificity must each be 95%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "New imaging techniques, such as MRI and electron-beam CT, must be relatively inexpensive and have excellent sensitivity and specificity to be cost-effective compared with other techniques for the diagnosis of coronary artery disease. Similar analyses in other areas of health care may help to focus the development of new diagnostic technology."
        }
      ]},
